[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-39749"] :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{3}-2$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{3}-2⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{3}-3)-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}\\cdot ({\\pi }^{3}-3)-{0}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}({\\pi }^{3}-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-98995"] :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}),t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+{x}^{2}y\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =x{y}^{2}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-104625/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "65ee09be-f50a-5efb-b77a-0e5717e3379d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104625/selection/7a1faa903ae46b0a3e4acf78c14a9880b73ef464bcd4b78c3e7a703c6538af45" :answer/id #uuid "d8de15ee-1e09-5e0a-b708-ef65b709f64b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104625/selection/0565f1f20ec34d894ed97519d097802ce8cfc0aa64c42aeae8bbf469ecaccfd7" :answer/id #uuid "a68d9a02-dbef-5dd5-bcbe-d152d98e32eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1+e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104625/selection/649a0c087fcc05f16d68429bd7ce66a20415f8fc960999bdbb5d26be6f9839da" :answer/id #uuid "27d29702-bb30-5b8c-9723-8aeb13ad95e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104625/selection/06754bd6f920861fd0cbf1f1a856873dccefbf5d93f630ed7fecfb9935f0c512" :answer/id #uuid "38a328c6-dc17-5bbe-8857-154506c513dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e-{e}^{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104625/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e737d57-b475-589a-ad59-4e2b310035ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104625/selection/7a1faa903ae46b0a3e4acf78c14a9880b73ef464bcd4b78c3e7a703c6538af45" :answer-field/choices ["q-104625/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-104625/selection/7a1faa903ae46b0a3e4acf78c14a9880b73ef464bcd4b78c3e7a703c6538af45" "q-104625/selection/0565f1f20ec34d894ed97519d097802ce8cfc0aa64c42aeae8bbf469ecaccfd7" "q-104625/selection/649a0c087fcc05f16d68429bd7ce66a20415f8fc960999bdbb5d26be6f9839da" "q-104625/selection/06754bd6f920861fd0cbf1f1a856873dccefbf5d93f630ed7fecfb9935f0c512"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104625" :question/id #uuid "e5a1609b-f9e4-55ff-a950-03f5b52f54d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-104625" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3}+1$ for $x\\in [1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{3}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,2)$ and $(2,9).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(2,9)-f(1,2) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot 1-{e}^{1}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-104625/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104625"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-104731/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "02599dd7-3059-5025-b594-e02eddc40139" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104731/selection/cee37ae0a2921bd3973a5cb0592c822dbbfafcfefba8d7007910ead797b9112f" :answer/id #uuid "a1dbaa58-cea8-5cd7-9f52-fdfd959d5c06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104731/selection/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd" :answer/id #uuid "c75e5132-e9c8-5830-b0ea-b7307fef3fa7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104731/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "aba82fcd-fc34-5a51-8171-230b06bada9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104731/selection/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" :answer/id #uuid "c8dce52f-ef25-5b79-9f9d-595d227c2f00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-104731/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "20c61aeb-762b-581e-8b5d-0aec98ee5142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-104731/selection/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" :answer-field/choices ["q-104731/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-104731/selection/cee37ae0a2921bd3973a5cb0592c822dbbfafcfefba8d7007910ead797b9112f" "q-104731/selection/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd" "q-104731/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-104731/selection/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-104731" :question/id #uuid "dc48dcc1-4f64-502c-acc3-48a296354b8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104731" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2t,{t}^{2}-1⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(2xy-y)\\,i+({x}^{2}-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2xy-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(4,3).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(4,3)-f(0,-1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 3)-({0}^{2}\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =48-12 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-104731/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-104731"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-124034/selection/2d423200ea52f8d1811b263b7d2b7e7fe459571df24e3ce9005e19ed496e12c3" :answer/id #uuid "19037058-b9b6-54eb-bd2f-e3337a19104b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124034/selection/cfd9d80e79ed6aaf485fc3ab0f251f7c0144756efd1898990322a23f8bb67d66" :answer/id #uuid "d15fca75-8925-5cb9-8917-46e36a98191e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1+\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124034/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "66142dfe-8517-5a0d-b903-31c4ebe88219" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124034/selection/33a0f30dd583c1bcda33d9114584d58f561892bcef517adbb92658101506fa3b" :answer/id #uuid "740e2bf3-edc9-5461-8b34-c7666d1850aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124034/selection/a35ede6ec6e48ccb90434c701b214a4130c697b9dcccfc110e1ef0284e549844" :answer/id #uuid "dc80d883-65bb-5ae8-8591-c2e7cc55abf0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-124034/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d78b716a-32a8-5831-a13e-9e2b296d092c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-124034/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer-field/choices ["q-124034/selection/2d423200ea52f8d1811b263b7d2b7e7fe459571df24e3ce9005e19ed496e12c3" "q-124034/selection/cfd9d80e79ed6aaf485fc3ab0f251f7c0144756efd1898990322a23f8bb67d66" "q-124034/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-124034/selection/33a0f30dd583c1bcda33d9114584d58f561892bcef517adbb92658101506fa3b" "q-124034/selection/a35ede6ec6e48ccb90434c701b214a4130c697b9dcccfc110e1ef0284e549844"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-124034" :question/id #uuid "d98baf4b-12a4-56ec-8adc-f870e121a413" :question/math-academy-id "q-124034" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $2x=\\pi {y}^{3}$ for $y\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{\\pi {t}^{3}}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡1-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-124034/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-124034"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
